find(b\"\\n\", start)\n            if end == -1:\n                chunks.append(data[start:])\n                break\n            segment = data[start:end]\n            if chunks:\n                chunks.append(segment)\n                line = b\"\".join(chunks)\n                chunks.clear()\n            else:\n                line = segment\n            if line.endswith(b\"\\r\"):\n                line = line[:-1]\n            if line and not line.isspace():\n                out.append(json.loads(line))\n            start = end + 1\n        return out\n```\n\nWait, bug: `if end == -1: chunks.append(data[start:]); break` — but `data[start:]` could be empty? If start < size, then data[start:] is non-empty. start<size guaranteed by loop condition. Good.\n\nAlso `line.endswith(b\"\\r\")` on empty bytes returns False, fine. Actually order: line=b'' → endswith false → `if line and ...` false → skip. Good. line=b'  ' → endswith false → line truthy, isspace() true → skip. line=b'  \\r' → strip \\r → b'  ' → isspace → skip. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `line.isspace()` on bytes with non-ASCII bytes? bytes.isspace() only considers ASCII whitespace bytes (space, \\t, \\n, \\r, \\v, \\f). For b'\\xc2\\xa0' returns False → json.loads raises UnicodeDecodeError? No — \\xc2\\xa0 is valid UTF-8 for U+00A0; json.loads gives error \"Expecting value\" JSONDecodeError. Fine.\n\nWhat about `json.loads` on bytes containing a BOM? Not relevant.\n\nNow, is there any reason to strip other whitespace before parse? json.loads handles leading/trailing ASCII whitespace itself.\n\nMemory: for a huge line, `b\"\".join(chunks)` once at completion — linear. Good.\n\nNow also think: should feed also accept `str`? If a hidden test feeds str, `data.find(b\"\\n\")` raises TypeError. But the task explicitly says byte chunks; test_public uses bytes. I'll support both cheaply? Adding `if isinstance(chunk, str): chunk = chunk.encode(\"utf-8\")` is a small robustness addition. Hmm, \"Do not add error handling... for scenarios that can't happen.\" The docstring says feeds receive byte chunks. I'll skip str support. Actually... let me reconsider: Some hidden tests might mix? Unlikely. The task statement: \"JsonlStream.feed receives arbitrary byte chunks from a network stream\". Bytes only. Keep it lean.\n\nOne more design question: Should parsing of a complete line that is invalid JSON raise immediately, or should we buffer? Raise is standard and matches original behavior.\n\nNow let me also double check public test passes: feed(b'{\"a\":1}\\n') → data size 8; find \\n at 7; segment b'{\"a\":1}'; chunks empty → line=segment; no \\r; truthy; json.loads → {\"a\":1}; out=[{\"a\":1}]. ✓\n\nLet me write tests of my own (not modifying test_public.py) — I can run an inline Python script via shell to verify chunk-boundary splits, codepoint splits, blank lines, multiple records, no premature emission, CRLF, and a fuzz test comparing against a reference implementation (feed byte-by-byte and compare with feeding whole). Also test invalid UTF-8 raising? And performance: feed 1-byte chunks of a 1MB record to ensure linear-ish.\n\nWhere to put a test script? Rules say don't create evaluator-bypass files; I can create a temporary script outside the repo (/tmp) to avoid polluting working tree, or run python -c inline. I'll run inline via `python3 -c` with a heredoc to avoid creating files in the repo. Note cwd /tmp/flashbench_deepseek_v41/t02.\n\nAlso, should `stream.py` keep the one-line class style? I'll write clean formatted code.\n\nLet me implement via write_file (full replacement). Also add docstring? \"NEVER add comments to code unless explicitly requested\" — system prompt says never add comments unless requested. But a docstring describing behavior may count as comment. Keep it minimal: maybe a single class docstring is acceptable? The rule says never add comments. I'll avoid comments; perhaps one short docstring is fine but let me follow the rule strictly and not add any comments/docstrings. Though production-quality usually has a docstring... The code_quality instruction: \"NEVER add comments to code unless explicitly requested by the user\". I'll keep it comment-free.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\n\nclass JsonlStream:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._partial = []\n\n    def feed(self, chunk):\n        out = []\n        data = chunk\n        partial = self._partial\n        start = 0\n        size = len(data)\n        while start < size:\n            end = data.find(b\"\\n\", start)\n            if end < 0:\n                partial.append(data[start:])\n                break\n            segment = data[start:end]\n            if partial:\n                partial.append(segment)\n                line = b\"\".join(partial)\n                partial.clear()\n            else:\n                line = segment\n            if line.endswith(b\"\\r\"):\n                line = line[:-1]\n            if line and not line.isspace():\n                out.append(json.loads(line))\n            start = end + 1\n        return out\n```\n\nHmm, `data = chunk` — keep reference. If chunk is a bytearray, `.find(b\"\\n\", start)` works, slicing gives bytearray, `b\"\".join(partial)` works with bytearray pieces too (join accepts bytes-like? b\"\".join accepts any bytes-like objects, yes, including bytearray). `line.endswith` works, `.isspace()` works. json.loads accepts bytearray. So actually bytearray input works fine even without conversion! memoryview: find exists on memoryview? No, memoryview has no find. Slicing memoryview works. But `isinstance` not needed. So our implementation handles bytes and bytearray naturally. Good, no conversion needed.\n\nWait, `data[start:end]` where data is bytearray returns bytearray; stored in partial; when joined with bytes fine. `out.append(json.loads(line))` line could be bytearray → accepted. \n\nNow think about the pathological many-newlines slicing: we don't re-slice `data` per iteration (we use