{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"Now let me verify with the public test plus my own comprehensive checks. I'll run the public test and a heredoc inline test.\n\nTest cases:\n1. Public: feed(b'{\"a\":1}\\n') == [{\"a\":1}]\n2. Record split: feed(b'{\"a\":') == [] ; feed(b'1}\\n') == [{\"a\":1}]\n3. UTF-8 codepoint split: b'{\"k\":\"\\xc3' then b'\\xa9\"}\\n' → [{\"k\":\"é\"}]\n4. Emoji split at every byte boundary.\n5. Blank lines ignored: b'\\n\\n \\t \\n'.\n6. Multiple records per chunk, records split across many chunks (byte-by-byte).\n7. CRLF.\n8. No premature emission of number prefix: b'12' then b'3\\n' → [123] not [12, 3].\n9. Fuzz: random JSON objects, serialize to lines, feed chunked at random boundaries; compare to reference list.\n10. Byte-by-byte feeding of a fuzz corpus compared to whole-feed result.\n11. Performance: 2MB record fed in 4KB chunks, time it (ensure not quadratic - could compare with timing rough).\n12. Invalid JSON raises JSONDecodeError? Let's check it raises.\n13. Empty chunk returns [].\n\nLet me build a test script inline. Use python3 - <<'EOF'."}}
